; clocks.pi4 - the Raspberry Pi 4 clock library
;
; THE PROBLEM THIS EXISTS TO SOLVE, measured on the board 2026-08-25:
;
;   the board file says   ClockSpeed = 1500000000
;   the machine boots at            600000000
;
; A factor of 2.5, with nothing anywhere saying so. `pi4ClockProbe`
; measured it, and this library is the other half of that finding.
;
; THE HOUSE CONVENTION, and this file follows it rather than inventing
; one. On the RP2350 the compiler does NOT raise the clock in the emitted
; startup - `clocks.pico2` provides `ClockInit()` and the PROGRAM calls
; it. F_CPU is understood to be the speed AFTER that call, not the speed
; out of reset. The Pi 4 was not an exception to that convention; it was
; simply missing its half of it. Now it has one:
;
;     ClockInit()          ; ARM to its maximum, once, at the top of Main
;
; WHY NOT DO IT AUTOMATICALLY IN _start. Three reasons, and the third is
; the one that decides it:
;   1. it needs the mailbox, which needs DRAM and a buffer - a great deal
;      of machinery to run before the programmer's first line;
;   2. an image entered by something other than the usual firmware (a
;      loader, another OS, a test harness) might have no working mailbox
;      at all, and a startup that hangs there is unrecoverable;
;   3. it would be a silent side effect. A program that draws 3x the
;      current the moment it starts should say so in its own source.
;
; WHAT RAISING THE CLOCK DOES *NOT* AFFECT: delay(), Micros(), millis()
; and every timeout in timer.pi4. Those run on CNTPCT_EL0, the
; architectural counter, fixed at 54 MHz and deliberately independent of
; the CPU. That independence was MEASURED, not assumed - pi4TimerProbe
; ran the same delay at 600 MHz and at 1.5 GHz and got 10800007 against
; 10800003 ticks, a difference of 74 nanoseconds across a 2.5x change.
; So this library makes code run faster; it does not make delays shorter.
;
; WHAT IT DOES AFFECT: anything that counts instructions instead of
; ticks. If you have hand-written a spin loop, this changes its duration
; by 2.5x. Use timer.pi4 instead - that is what it is for.
;
; THE FIRMWARE CLAMPS RATHER THAN REFUSING, and every procedure here is
; shaped around that. Ask for 2.5 GHz on a 1.5 GHz part and the response
; code is SUCCESS, the tag is marked answered, and the rate you get is
; 1500000000. So every setter here RETURNS THE RATE THE FIRMWARE APPLIED
; and never a bare 1 for "worked". A wrapper that reported the rate it
; ASKED for would be a confident wrong answer about the speed of the
; machine, and every timing calculation downstream would inherit it.
;
; AND THE CEILING IS config.txt's, not the silicon's. `arm_freq` /
; `arm_freq_max` bound what the firmware will allow; this tag cannot
; exceed them. ClockMaxHz() reports what the firmware says it will give,
; which is the only number worth asking for.
;
; THE GOVERNOR CAN UNDO THIS AT ANY MOMENT. The on-demand governor moves
; the ARM clock between min and max by itself, for thermal and
; undervoltage reasons, with no notification. That is why
; ClockMeasuredHz() exists and why ClockInit() uses it to report success:
; the CONFIGURED rate is an intention and only the MEASURED rate is an
; observation.
;
; DEPENDS ON mailbox.pi4. Every clock on this part is the VideoCore's to
; hand out - there is no PLL register here to poke the way there is on an
; RP2040. Include mailbox.pi4 before this file.
;
; MEASURED ON THE BOARD, 2026-08-25, Pi 4 Model B rev 1.2, 4 GB:
;
;   clock  configured      min          max        measured
;   ARM     600000000   600000000   1500000000    600169920
;   CORE    200000000   200000000    500000000    200008304
;   EMMC    250000000   250000000    250000000    250000496
;   UART     48000000           0   1000000000     48001464
;
; The UART figure is worth keeping: it matches config.txt's
; init_uart_clock=48000000 AND a console that is visibly working at
; 115200, so the mailbox and the serial port corroborate each other.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  CLOCK IDS LIVE IN mailbox.pi4 AND ARE NOT REPEATED HERE.
;
;  The first draft of this file defined #CLK_ARM and friends as aliases
;  of #MBX_CLK_ARM. The compiler refuses a constant defined from another
;  constant, which turned out to be a favour: the alternative was
;  re-typing the four numbers, and two spellings of one number is exactly
;  how they drift apart. Use #MBX_CLK_ARM / _CORE / _EMMC / _UART
;  directly - they are one definition, in the file that owns the tag that
;  consumes them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

Global clk_lastApplied.i
Global clk_ready.i

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ClockHz(id)          the rate the firmware is CONFIGURED for
;  ClockMeasuredHz(id)  the rate it OBSERVES - the honest one
;  ClockMinHz / ClockMaxHz   the bounds config.txt allows
;
;  All four return 0 on a failed transaction; call MailboxError() for the
;  reason.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ClockHz(id.i)
  ProcedureReturn MailboxClockRate(id)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ClockMeasuredHz(id.i)
  ProcedureReturn MailboxClockRateMeasured(id)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ClockMinHz(id.i)
  ProcedureReturn MailboxClockRateMin(id)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ClockMaxHz(id.i)
  ProcedureReturn MailboxClockRateMax(id)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ClockSetHz(id, hz) - ask for a rate, get told what you actually got.
;
;  RETURNS THE APPLIED RATE, or 0 if the transaction failed. It is never
;  a boolean, for the clamping reason in the header: the difference
;  between what you asked for and what you got is the only interesting
;  part, and a boolean throws it away.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ClockSetHz(id.i, hz.i)
  Define applied.i
  applied = MailboxClockRateSet(id, hz, 0)
  clk_lastApplied = applied
  ProcedureReturn applied
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ClockInit() - THE ONE EVERY PROGRAM SHOULD CALL.
;
;  Raises the ARM clock to whatever maximum the firmware reports, so that
;  the machine actually runs at the speed the board file declares.
;
;  RETURNS THE MEASURED RATE IN Hz, and 0 if it could not be done. The
;  measured rate rather than the applied one, deliberately: the governor
;  can walk the clock back down between the two calls, and a program that
;  cares enough to raise the clock is a program that should be told when
;  the raise did not stick.
;
;  Safe to call more than once.
;
;  On the bench board this takes it from 600000000 to a measured
;  1500345728.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ClockInit()
  Define mx.i
  Define applied.i
  Define got.i

  clk_ready = 0
  If MailboxReady() = 0
    If MailboxInit() = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  EndIf

  mx = MailboxClockRateMax(#MBX_CLK_ARM)
  If mx = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  applied = ClockSetHz(#MBX_CLK_ARM, mx)
  If applied = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  got = MailboxClockRateMeasured(#MBX_CLK_ARM)
  If got > 0
    clk_ready = 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn got
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ClockInitHz(hz) - the same, but at a rate you name.
;
;  For a program that wants a KNOWN speed rather than the fastest one -
;  a repeatable benchmark, or a thermal budget. Put the number in a
;  constant in your own file so it is visible and adjustable:
;
;      #ARM_HZ = 1000000000
;      ...
;      ClockInitHz(#ARM_HZ)
;
;  Returns the measured rate. COMPARE IT WITH WHAT YOU ASKED FOR - the
;  firmware clamps silently, so a return value below your request means
;  config.txt's arm_freq_max is lower than you think, and a return value
;  below the APPLIED rate means the governor moved it afterwards.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ClockInitHz(hz.i)
  Define applied.i

  clk_ready = 0
  If MailboxReady() = 0
    If MailboxInit() = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  EndIf

  applied = ClockSetHz(#MBX_CLK_ARM, hz)
  If applied = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  clk_ready = 1
  ProcedureReturn MailboxClockRateMeasured(#MBX_CLK_ARM)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ClockApplied() - the rate the last ClockSetHz was given by the
;  firmware, without another mailbox round trip. 0 if none has run.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ClockApplied()
  ProcedureReturn clk_lastApplied
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ClockReady() - 1 once a ClockInit/ClockInitHz has completed.
;
;  It does NOT mean the clock is still where it was put. Nothing can mean
;  that on a part with an on-demand governor; ask ClockMeasuredHz().
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ClockReady()
  ProcedureReturn clk_ready
EndProcedure
